@inherits LayoutComponentBase
@implements IDisposable
@inject CharacterSession Session
@inject Theme Theme
@inject Motion Motion
@inject NavigationManager Nav
@inject Accounts Accounts

@* The fill runs the full width of the window; the contents sit on --column, like the two bands
   below it and the shell below them. That is one element rather than a `calc()` on the padding
   because it makes the banner structurally the same as the others, so one guard covers all four
   — see the comment on `.banner-inner` in app.css. *@
<header class="banner">
  <div class="banner-inner">
    <div class="banner-title">
        Prowlers &amp; Paragons
        <small>@(Where == Area.Portfolio ? "How this was built" : $"Character creation — {(Session.Mode == SheetMode.Hero ? "Hero" : "Villain")}")</small>
    </div>

    @* The door between the two halves of the site, and it says which one you are not in.

       It used to say "Watch one being built" from everywhere, which named a *demonstration*
       from inside the tool — so the only cross-link a player ever saw pointed away from what
       they were doing, at somebody else's conversation. Now the tool offers the portfolio and
       the portfolio offers the tool, which is the one thing each half wants from the other. *@
    @if (Where == Area.Portfolio)
    {
        <NavLink class="banner-link" href="">Build a character</NavLink>
    }
    else
    {
        <NavLink class="banner-link" href="portfolio">How this was built</NavLink>
    }

    @* The account, in the one place it is visible from every page.

       It names whoever is signed in rather than only offering a door, because the character on
       screen belongs to somebody and a shared machine is where that matters. Anonymous is the
       app's ordinary state, not a state to be nagged about, so the offer is a link and there is
       no banner, no badge and no interruption. *@
    <NavLink class="banner-link" href="signin">
        @(_who?.IsSignedIn == true ? _who.DisplayName : "Sign in")
    </NavLink>

    @* aria-pressed is written out as a string on purpose. Blazor drops a bool attribute
       when it is false and renders it as `aria-pressed=""` when it is true, and an empty
       value is invalid ARIA that assistive technology reads as *not* pressed — so the
       natural spelling announces the exact opposite of the state on both buttons. *@
    <div class="mode-switch" role="group" aria-label="Hero or Villain">
        <button type="button"
                aria-pressed="@Pressed(SheetMode.Hero)"
                @onclick="() => SetMode(SheetMode.Hero)">Hero</button>
        <button type="button"
                aria-pressed="@Pressed(SheetMode.Villain)"
                @onclick="() => SetMode(SheetMode.Villain)">Villain</button>
    </div>

    @* Light or dark, beside the identity switch because both are the palette and a reader
       looking for one will look here for the other.

       **Three buttons rather than a two-state toggle**, because there are three states and the
       third is not "off": following the system is a live setting that changes with the machine,
       and a toggle can only ever land somebody on whichever value their system held at the
       moment they touched it. "Auto" is the word for it on the screen; `system` is the word
       everywhere else, and they are the same state.

       aria-pressed is a string here for the reason the switch above gives. *@
    <div class="theme-switch" role="group" aria-label="Light or dark">
        <button type="button"
                aria-pressed="@Chose(ThemeChoice.Light)"
                @onclick="() => SetTheme(ThemeChoice.Light)">Light</button>
        <button type="button"
                aria-pressed="@Chose(ThemeChoice.Dark)"
                @onclick="() => SetTheme(ThemeChoice.Dark)">Dark</button>
        <button type="button"
                aria-pressed="@Chose(ThemeChoice.System)"
                @onclick="() => SetTheme(ThemeChoice.System)">Auto</button>
    </div>
  </div>
</header>

@* **The chrome is outside the shell, and that is what makes it one band rather than three.**
   The banner, the step list and the budget strip used to be three separately-framed
   horizontal things — the banner full width, then a step list with its own bottom rule inside
   the 1100px column, then the budget as a shadowed white card inset from the window. About
   215px of furniture before the page heading, on every step, reading as four stacked pieces.

   Out here they are full-window-width bands like the banner, contiguous, each centring its own
   contents on --column so that all of them agree — **including the banner, which this note
   originally claimed was already doing so and was not.** It had a flat side padding, so the
   wordmark sat 24px from the window edge while the step labels below it sat on the column: the
   one band described as the example was the only one out of step. The strip's negative-margin
   bleed is gone with them:
   it existed only to escape the shell's padding, and an element that is not inside the shell
   has nothing to escape. So has the pair of narrow-viewport rules that had to be kept in step
   with it, and the 8px overflow they caused at 375px is now unreachable rather than guarded.

   **The budget has to be a sibling of `main` rather than a child of a wrapper around the two
   bands.** `position: sticky` is bounded by its parent, so a short `.chrome` div holding both
   rows would unstick the strip the moment the band scrolled past — which is the whole span it
   exists to survive. Out here its containing block is the document, and it stays for the page.

   The nav also stops being inside `<main>`, which it should never have been. *@
@* **The six steps belong to the tool and are drawn only there.** They were drawn everywhere,
   including over a recording of somebody else's conversation — where a numbered list of steps
   the reader is not taking, with one of them marked as the step they are on, is an offer to
   continue something they are not doing. The budget strip below has always been hidden here for
   the same reason; the band above it simply had not been. *@
@if (Where == Area.Play)
{
    <StepNav />
}

@* The budget bar is the character being built, and it does not belong above a recording
   of somebody else's — nor above the page that says who may have an account, which is not a
   character at all. Both are six labelled figures in the same format — Package, Abilities,
   Talents, Powers, Perks, Gear — so a visitor part-way through their own character met
   "105 of 125 spent, 20 remaining" directly above a recorded character costed at 136, with
   nothing on the page saying whose was whose. Worse on the Villain recording, which
   deliberately shows no budget of its own: the only budget on the screen belonged to a
   different character entirely. *@
@if (Where == Area.Play)
{
    <HpBudgetBar />
}

<main class="shell">
    @Body
</main>

@* Ctrl-K, from every route including the recordings. It is a sibling of the shell rather than
   a child of it for the same reason the budget strip is one: it covers the window, and an
   overlay bounded by a padded column is an overlay with a margin.

   Rendered by the layout so the shortcut is reliable. A palette that existed on some steps and
   not others would be worse than none — a reader stops reaching for a key that sometimes does
   nothing, which costs the affordance everywhere rather than only where it is missing. *@
<CommandPalette />

@code {
    private string Pressed(SheetMode mode) => Session.Mode == mode ? "true" : "false";

    /// <summary>Whether the light/dark control shows this choice as the current one.</summary>
    private string Chose(ThemeChoice choice) => Theme.Choice == choice ? "true" : "false";

    /// <summary>
    /// The light/dark switch.
    ///
    /// <para>Unlike <see cref="SetMode"/> this does push to the document itself, and the
    /// difference is real rather than an inconsistency: the palette follows the <em>character</em>
    /// and so has to be applied on any render that changes which character is being built, while
    /// the theme follows the <em>reader</em> and changes on nothing but this click. There is one
    /// route into each and neither has two.</para>
    /// </summary>
    private Task SetTheme(ThemeChoice choice) => Theme.Choose(choice).AsTask();

    /// <summary>
    /// Which of the two things this site is the reader is currently in.
    ///
    /// <para>Read from the address rather than passed down, because the shell renders the body and
    /// cannot ask it anything. <see cref="Areas.Of"/> owns the matching, including the
    /// case-insensitivity that Blazor's own routing has and an ordinal comparison here would
    /// not.</para>
    /// </summary>
    private Area Where => Areas.Of(Nav.ToBaseRelativePath(Nav.Uri));

    /// <summary>Who the banner names, or null until the first answer arrives.</summary>
    private Identity? _who;

    /// <summary>
    /// The name in the banner follows a sign-in without a reload.
    ///
    /// <para>Asked once here and then re-asked when <see cref="Accounts"/> says it has changed —
    /// which is the whole reason identity is a service that can change its mind rather than a
    /// value fetched at startup.</para>
    /// </summary>
    /// <summary>
    /// Who the banner names, and which light/dark choice its control shows as current.
    ///
    /// <para>The theme itself is already on the document by now — <c>js/theme.js</c> stamped it
    /// before the first paint, seconds before this ran. What is read here is only what the
    /// <em>control</em> should show, which is why nothing flickers when it arrives.</para>
    /// </summary>
    protected override async Task OnInitializedAsync()
    {
        _who = await Accounts.CurrentAsync();
        await Theme.ReadChoice();
    }

    private void WhoChanged() => _ = InvokeAsync(async () =>
    {
        _who = await Accounts.CurrentAsync();
        StateHasChanged();
    });

    protected override void OnInitialized()
    {
        Session.Changed += StateHasChanged;
        Accounts.Changed += WhoChanged;

        // The shell has to redraw on a navigation as well as on a change to the character:
        // walking from a recording back into the wizard has to bring the budget bar back.
        Nav.LocationChanged += Moved;

        // ...and a view transition has to be *opened* before the navigation, which is a
        // different hook entirely. See OpenTransition.
        _leaving = Nav.RegisterLocationChangingHandler(OpenTransition);
    }

    private void Moved(object? sender, LocationChangedEventArgs e) => StateHasChanged();

    /// <summary>The LocationChanging registration, released on dispose.</summary>
    private IDisposable? _leaving;

    /// <summary>Whether a transition is open and waiting on this render to finish.</summary>
    private bool _midTransition;

    /// <summary>The palette last pushed onto the document element, or null before the first.</summary>
    private SheetMode? _appliedPalette;

    /// <summary>
    /// Snapshot the page before Blazor swaps the step out from under the chrome.
    ///
    /// <para><b><c>LocationChanged</c> is the wrong hook for this and it is the obvious one to
    /// reach for.</b> The View Transitions API animates between two snapshots and the first has
    /// to be taken while the old page is still on screen; by the time <c>LocationChanged</c>
    /// fires, the navigation has happened and there is nothing left to capture.
    /// <c>LocationChanging</c> runs before it.</para>
    ///
    /// <para><b>Nothing here decides whether to animate.</b> The script asks the browser whether
    /// it supports transitions and the user whether they want movement, and does nothing at all
    /// if either answer is no — a component can ask neither question, and the reduced-motion
    /// tokens in the stylesheet cannot reach a script.</para>
    /// </summary>
    private async ValueTask OpenTransition(LocationChangingContext context)
    {
        _ = context;    // nothing here ever cancels a navigation; this only observes one

        _midTransition = true;
        await Motion.Begin();
    }

    /// <summary>
    /// Release the transition once the new step has actually rendered, which is what lets the
    /// second snapshot be taken.
    ///
    /// <para>Guarded on the flag because this fires after <em>every</em> render — a rank
    /// changing, the breakdown opening — and an unguarded release would close the next
    /// navigation's transition before its content had arrived.</para>
    /// </summary>
    protected override async Task OnAfterRenderAsync(bool firstRender)
    {
        // **The palette follows the character, and not only on a click.** It lives on the sheet
        // now, so it changes whenever *which character is being built* changes — restored from
        // storage on the first render, loaded as a sample, taken out of a recording, or started
        // again. `data-mode` is on the document element, outside the component tree, so nothing
        // else can push it.
        //
        // Guarded on what was last pushed rather than on `firstRender`: first render alone
        // misses the other three, and pushing on every render would send an interop call for
        // each rank a reader clicks. The comparison is what makes it both complete and quiet.
        if (_appliedPalette != Session.Mode)
        {
            _appliedPalette = Session.Mode;
            await ApplyPalette();
        }

        if (!_midTransition) return;

        _midTransition = false;
        await Motion.End();
    }

    /// <summary>
    /// The Hero/Villain switch. It records the choice on the character and stops there.
    ///
    /// <para>The palette is a <c>data-mode</c> attribute on the document element, set through
    /// interop rather than by re-rendering: it has to reach index.html's own markup, which is
    /// outside the component tree. <see cref="ApplyPalette"/> is where that happens.</para>
    ///
    /// <para><b>It deliberately does not push the palette itself.</b> Setting the mode raises the
    /// session's change event, which redraws this component, which applies the palette on the way
    /// out — the same path a restored character, a loaded sample and a character taken from a
    /// recording all take. Pushing here as well would be a second route to the same thing, and a
    /// second route is where the two get to disagree.</para>
    /// </summary>
    private void SetMode(SheetMode mode) => Session.Mode = mode;

    /// <summary>
    /// Push the character's own palette onto the document element.
    ///
    /// <para>Read from the session rather than passed in, so the two callers cannot disagree:
    /// one is a click on the switch and the other is the first render after a character has been
    /// restored, loaded as a sample, or arrived from a recording. Anything that changes which
    /// character is being built has to come through here, or the colours are the last thing
    /// somebody clicked rather than the character on screen.</para>
    /// </summary>
    private ValueTask ApplyPalette() => Theme.Apply(Session.Mode);

    public void Dispose()
    {
        Session.Changed -= StateHasChanged;
        Accounts.Changed -= WhoChanged;
        Nav.LocationChanged -= Moved;
        _leaving?.Dispose();
    }
}
